			en: `The map and charts use the raw annual loss rate, in %/year, without normalisation: bands ≤0.5 low, 0.5–2 medium, >2 high. Source: Hansen Global Forest Change v1.12 (University of Maryland / Google), derived from Landsat time series at 30m, coverage 2001–2024. True zonal statistics: each H3 resolution 9 hexagon (~0.1 km²) receives the pixel count of annual loss within its polygon (not centroid). The rate is the fraction of pixels with detected loss in the period divided by its years (10 %/year = the whole hexagon lost over the period). Baseline: 2001–2010; current: 2015–2024. Cumulative loss: % of the hexagon with loss in 2001–2024. Hansen publishes one release a year; each new release is incorporated by hand in every province, department and state. Department ranking, three readings of the same loss: hectares lost per year in 2015–2024, % of the year-2000 tree cover lost per year, and change from 2001–2010 (only where 2001–2010 loss exceeded 50 ha/yr). Hansen counts plantations as forest: in Misiones, Corrientes and Rio Grande do Sul part of the loss is pine and eucalyptus harvest.`,
